. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * To identify the causal effect of having a daughter on political pre
> ferences, we use the panel
.         * structure of the data and employ a difference-in-differences (DiD) 
> approach. Specifically, we
.         * compare within-individual changes in outcomes before and after the 
> birth of a child between
.         * two groups: parents whose first child is a daughter (treatment grou
> p) and those whose first
.         * child is a son (control group). As the gender of the first-born chi
> ld is random by nature, we
.         * can exploit this exogenous variation to identify causal treatment e
> ffects. We use a Two-Way-
.         * Fixed-Effect setting accounting for individual, household, and surv
> ey year fixed effects. To
.         * measure political preference, we use a variable in which individual
> s indicate their political
.         * views on a Likert scale ranging from 0 (far left) to 10 (far right)
> .
.         
.         * CHANGED SPECIFICATION: In the PAP (as writte above) we wanted to co
> ntrol for household fixed effects. This
.         * can not be done, as the variable of interest (daughter) is constant
>  across households and would therefore
.         * be fully absorbed in the household fixed effects. For the regressio
> n model we corrected this and REMOVED
.         * the household fixed effects
.         
.         xtset pid syear

Panel variable: pid (unbalanced)
 Time variable: syear, 2005 to 2019, but with gaps
         Delta: 1 unit

